        "1": {
          "id": "1",
          "name": "index.html",
          "language": "html",
          "contents": "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n  <meta charset=\"utf-8\" />\n  <title>Lesson 8 · Playlist Organizer (Filter Logic)</title>\n  <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\" />\n  <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"style.css\" />\n  <script defer src=\"script.js\"></script>\n</head>\n<body>\n  <div class=\"wrap\">\n    <header>\n      <h1>Playlist Organizer · Filter Logic Patterns</h1>\n      <p>Explore how filter functions can shape which songs users see — and what patterns make filters fair and reliable.</p>\n      <div class=\"toolbar\">\n        <label><input type=\"checkbox\" id=\"toggle-contrast\" /> High contrast</label>\n        <label><input type=\"checkbox\" id=\"big-text\" /> Larger text</label>\n      </div>\n    </header>\n\n    <div class=\"grid\">\n      <main class=\"panel\" id=\"left\">\n        <h2>Code Output</h2>\n        <p class=\"muted\">Students will edit the filter logic directly in <code>script.js</code>. The results of their function appear below.</p>\n        <section id=\"feed\" class=\"feed\"></section>\n      </main>\n\n      <aside class=\"panel\" id=\"right\">\n        <h2>ECHO: Filter Logic Patterns</h2>\n\n        <h3>Explore</h3>\n        <p>Ask AI: “Show me two ways to write a filter function that returns only songs where <code>genre === \"Pop\"</code>.”</p>\n\n        <h3>Co-Create</h3>\n        <p>Experiment with variations. Try adding multiple genres or handling missing fields.</p>\n\n        <h3>Highlights</h3>\n        <ul class=\"small\">\n          <li>Always start with an empty array (<code>const results = []</code>).</li>\n          <li>Use clear, readable condition names.</li>\n          <li>Handle missing or undefined values gracefully.</li>\n          <li>Keep logic flexible for reuse.</li>\n        </ul>\n\n        <h3>Own</h3>\n        <p>Write your own function — for example <code>filterByGenre(songs, \"Indie\")</code> — and test it below!</p>\n      </aside>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</body>\n</html>\n",
          "active": false,
          "folderId": "0"
        },
        "2": {
          "id": "2",
          "name": "style.css",
          "language": "css",
          "contents": ":root {\n  --bg: #fafafa;\n  --text: #222;\n  --muted: #666;\n  --accent: #0077cc;\n  --border: #ddd;\n  --pill-bg: #e0f0ff;\n  --pill-text: #004b80;\n}\n\nbody {\n  font-family: system-ui, sans-serif;\n  margin: 0;\n  background: var(--bg);\n  color: var(--text);\n  line-height: 1.5;\n}\n\n.wrap {\n  padding: 1rem 2rem;\n}\n\nheader {\n  margin-bottom: 1rem;\n}\n\n.grid {\n  display: grid;\n  grid-template-columns: 2fr 1fr;\n  gap: 1rem;\n}\n\n.panel {\n  border: 1px solid var(--border);\n  border-radius: 8px;\n  padding: 1rem;\n  background: white;\n}\n\n.feed {\n  display: grid;\n  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(200px, 1fr));\n  gap: 0.75rem;\n  margin-top: 1rem;\n}\n\n.card {\n  border: 1px solid var(--border);\n  border-radius: 8px;\n  padding: 0.75rem;\n  background: #fff;\n  transition: box-shadow 0.2s;\n}\n.card:hover {\n  box-shadow: 0 2px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);\n}\n\n.muted {\n  color: var(--muted);\n}\n\n.toolbar {\n  margin-top: 0.5rem;\n}\n\n.high-contrast body {\n  background: black;\n  color: white;\n}\n\n.big-text {\n  font-size: 1.2em;\n}\n",
          "folderId": "0",
          "active": false
        },
        "3": {
          "id": "3",
          "name": "script.js",
          "language": "js",
          "contents": "// --- Dataset ---\nconst SONGS = [\n  { title: \"Skyline Drive\", artist: \"Nova\", genre: \"Pop\", duration: 210 },\n  { title: \"Midnight Circuit\", artist: \"Axel\", genre: \"EDM\", duration: 189 },\n  { title: \"Golden Days\", artist: \"River\", genre: \"Rock\", duration: 235 },\n  { title: \"Velvet Bloom\", artist: \"Nova\", genre: \"Indie\", duration: 202 },\n  { title: \"Pulse Runner\", artist: \"Max\", genre: \"Pop\", duration: 178 },\n  { title: \"Shoreline\", artist: \"Emi\", genre: \"\", duration: 199 }\n];\n\nconst feed = document.getElementById(\"feed\");\n\n// --- Example 1: Basic filter (Explore step) ---\n// Use .map() and conditionals to select only Pop songs\nconst filterPopSongs = (list) => {\n  const results = [];\n  list.map(song => {\n    if (song.genre === \"Pop\") {\n      results.push(song);\n    }\n  });\n  return results;\n};\n\n// --- Example 2: More flexible (Co-Create step) ---\n// Handle multiple genres and missing fields\nconst filterByGenres = (list, genres) => {\n  const results = [];\n  list.map(song => {\n    if (song.genre && genres.includes(song.genre)) {\n      results.push(song);\n    }\n  });\n  return results;\n};\n\n// --- Student Zone (Own step) ---\n// Students can modify this using only .map() and conditionals\nconst myFilterFunction = (list) => {\n  const results = [];\n  list.map(song => {\n    if (song.artist === \"Nova\" || song.genre === \"Rock\") {\n      results.push(song);\n    }\n  });\n  return results;\n};\n\n// --- Render Function ---\nconst renderResults = (results, label) => {\n  const section = document.createElement(\"section\");\n  const heading = document.createElement(\"h3\");\n  heading.textContent = label;\n  section.appendChild(heading);\n\n  if (results.length === 0) {\n    const message = document.createElement(\"p\");\n    message.className = \"muted\";\n    message.textContent = \"No matches found.\";\n    section.appendChild(message);\n  } else {\n    const grid = document.createElement(\"div\");\n    grid.className = \"feed\";\n\n    // Use .map() again to display songs\n    results.map(song => {\n      const card = document.createElement(\"div\");\n      card.className = \"card\";\n      card.innerHTML = `\n        <h4>${song.title}</h4>\n        <p><strong>Artist:</strong> ${song.artist}</p>\n        <p><strong>Genre:</strong> ${song.genre || \"N/A\"}</p>\n        <p><strong>Duration:</strong> ${song.duration}s</p>\n      `;\n      grid.appendChild(card);\n    });\n\n    section.appendChild(grid);\n  }\n  feed.appendChild(section);\n};\n\n// --- Run Tests ---\nfeed.innerHTML = \"\";\nrenderResults(filterPopSongs(SONGS), \"Explore: Pop Songs\");\nrenderResults(filterByGenres(SONGS, [\"Pop\", \"Rock\"]), \"Co-Create: Pop + Rock\");\nrenderResults(myFilterFunction(SONGS), \"Own: Student Custom Filter\");\n\n// --- Accessibility toggles ---\ndocument.getElementById(\"toggle-contrast\").addEventListener(\"change\", (e) => {\n  document.body.classList.toggle(\"high-contrast\", e.target.checked);\n});\n\ndocument.getElementById(\"big-text\").addEventListener(\"change\", (e) => {\n  document.body.classList.toggle(\"big-text\", e.target.checked);\n});\n",
          "folderId": "0",
          "active": false
        },
        "4": {
          "id": "4",
          "name": "progression.md",
          "language": "md",
          "contents": "\n# Lesson 8 · Campus Playlist Organizer  \n## Filtering\n\n**Question of the Day:**  \nHow can filter functions help users explore collections fairly and effectively?\n\n**Objectives:**  \n- Implement a filter function that returns a subset of data.  \n- Debug a filter function.  \n- Validate a filter function with AI-generated test cases and refine them to address bias or missing coverage.  \n\n---\n\n## 🎧 Scenario  \n\nYou’re part of the **Campus Playlist Organizer** team.  \nYour web app shows a shared playlist with songs from different genres and artists.  \nYour goal is to build filter logic that lets users explore specific genres or artists — and to reflect on how that logic might include or exclude content.  \n\n### Sample Dataset  \n\n```js\nconst SONGS = [\n  { title: \"Skyline Drive\", artist: \"Nova\", genre: \"Pop\",   duration: 210 },\n  { title: \"Midnight Circuit\", artist: \"Axel\", genre: \"EDM\", duration: 189 },\n  { title: \"Golden Days\", artist: \"River\", genre: \"Rock\",   duration: 235 },\n  { title: \"Velvet Bloom\", artist: \"Nova\", genre: \"Indie\",  duration: 202 },\n  { title: \"Pulse Runner\", artist: \"Max\",  genre: \"Pop\",    duration: 178 },\n  { title: \"Shoreline\",    artist: \"Emi\",  genre: \"\",       duration: 199 }\n];\n```\n\n---\n\n## 🧭 ECHO Framework Cycle — *Filter Logic Patterns*  \n\n### **E · Explore**  \nStudents prompt AI:  \n> “Show me two different ways to write a filter function that returns only songs where genre === 'Pop'.”  \n\nAI may produce two versions — for example:  \n1️⃣ A basic `for` loop that pushes matches into a new array.  \n2️⃣ A `.filter()` function with a conditional that checks `genre`.  \n\nStudents run each and observe how missing or undefined genres affect results.  \n\n---\n\n### **C · Co-Create**  \nStudents ask clarifying questions and experiment with variations:  \n> “Can you make this version show both Pop and Rock songs?”  \n> “What happens if a song has no genre value?”  \n\nThey tweak AI outputs and compare results for accuracy and fairness.  \n\n---\n\n### **H · Highlights**  \nStudents identify and document good filtering practices:  \n- Always start with an empty array (`const results = []`).  \n- Use clear, readable conditions.  \n- Handle missing or undefined values gracefully.  \n- Keep logic flexible and reusable for different fields.  \n\nThese highlights are recorded in the reflection panel of the prototype.  \n\n---\n\n### **O · Own**  \nStudents write and test their own function, such as:  \n\n```js\nfunction filterByGenre(songs, genre) {\n  return songs.filter(song =>\n    song.genre &&\n    song.genre.toLowerCase() === genre.toLowerCase()\n  );\n}\n```\n\nThey validate their code by creating AI-generated test cases like “missing genre,” “multiple artists,” and “no matches,” then record what happens.  \n\n---\n\n## ✅ Debug & Validation Step  \n\nStudents use AI to generate datasets with edge cases and predict expected results, then compare predicted vs actual outputs to debug.  \n\nCommon tests:  \n- Songs missing a genre property.  \n- Genres with different capitalization (`\"pop\"` vs `\"Pop\"`).  \n- Artists with shared names.  \n- No songs meeting criteria.  \n\n---\n\n## 💼 Career Connection  \n\n**Music Data Curator** — Ensures metadata is complete and representative across genres.  \n**Front-End Developer** — Builds filters that are fast, transparent, and inclusive.  \n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Reflection Prompts  \n\n- “How could a filter function accidentally hide certain artists or genres?”  \n- “What patterns make a filter more inclusive and flexible?”  \n- “How can AI help you test for bias or missing coverage?”  \n\n---\n\n## 🧩 Teacher Notes / Flow  \n\n| Step | Focus | Student Action | AI Interaction |  \n|:--|:--|:--|:--|  \n| Explore | Compare AI examples | Run and observe two versions of filter logic | Prompt AI for code variants |  \n| Co-Create | Modify logic | Ask follow-up questions, edit conditions | Iteratively prompt and test |  \n| Highlights | Analyze patterns | List best practices from examples | Summarize AI insights |  \n| Own | Apply and validate | Write personal filter function, debug with AI tests | Generate test cases |  \n\n---\n\n## 📁 Files in Prototype  \n\n- `index.html` — UI scaffold and sidebar with ECHO steps.  \n- `style.css` — Shared visual theme from Lesson 7.  \n- `script.js` — Dataset and editable filter functions for Explore, Co-Create, and Own steps.  \n",
          "folderId": "0",
          "active": false
        },
        "5": {
          "id": "5",
          "name": "Highlights.md",
          "language": "md",
          "contents": "\n# Lesson 8 · Playlist Organizer  \n## 🟦 Highlight Phase — Reflection Notes  \n\n**Goal:** Identify patterns and strong practices you noticed while testing or reading different versions of filter logic.  \n\n---\n\n### 🧠 What You Observed  \n\n| Observation | Example or Evidence | Why It Matters |\n|:--|:--|:--|\n| Some filters returned `undefined` when no match was found. | In one version, songs without a genre made the array include blank spots. | This can confuse users because the UI might show “empty cards.” |\n| A clear conditional (`if (song.genre === \"Pop\")`) was easier to understand than nested checks. | The AI’s first example used `if (song.genre && song.genre === \"Pop\")`, which felt longer but safer. | Readable conditionals help debugging later. |\n| Functions that used lowercase comparisons (`toLowerCase()`) worked better across cases. | When I typed `\"pop\"` instead of `\"Pop\"`, the lowercase version still matched correctly. | Makes the function more inclusive and reliable. |\n| Starting with an empty array made the logic easier to follow. | The AI used `const results = []` before mapping. | Keeps your code predictable and easy to expand. |\n| Handling missing fields prevented errors. | One song had no genre (`\"\"`), but some versions crashed without that check. | Defensive code keeps apps accessible and stable. |\n\n---\n\n### 🪄 My “Rules of Thumb”  \n\nWrite three *rules of thumb* (short guiding principles) that summarize what you learned about effective filtering:  \n\n1. Always check that the property exists before comparing.  \n2. Use consistent lowercase comparisons for fairness and inclusivity.  \n3. Keep conditions simple and easy to read — one idea per `if`.  \n\n---\n\n### 💬 Reflection Prompt  \n\n> How could filter logic unintentionally leave out songs, artists, or genres?  \n> What’s one change you’d make to make your filters more fair?  \n\n**Sample student reflection:**  \n> “I realized that filters can unintentionally ignore songs without a genre, even if they’re valid entries. I’d change my code to include them with a label like ‘Uncategorized’ so users still see them.”  \n\n---\n\n### 🧩 Bonus: Connect to Practice  \n\n| Role | What This Skill Looks Like in That Career |\n|:--|:--|\n| **Front-End Developer** | Writes filters that are clear, consistent, and easy to maintain. |\n| **Data Analyst** | Uses conditions to segment data fairly without bias. |\n| **UX Designer** | Ensures users understand what’s being filtered out or included. |\n\n---\n\n### ✅ My Next Step  \n\n- [ ] Rewrite my filter function with improved readability.  \n- [ ] Add a check for missing or undefined data.  \n- [ ] Write comments explaining each conditional.  \n\n---\n\n### 🧾 Optional Extension  \n\nTry turning your highlights into a *coding guideline* your team could reuse in the next project.  \n\n**Example:**  \n> “When filtering data, always handle missing values, use lowercase checks, and name your conditions clearly.”\n",
          "folderId": "0",
          "active": false
        },
        "6": {
          "id": "6",
          "name": "Highlights-v2.md",
          "language": "md",
          "contents": "\n# Lesson 8 · Playlist Organizer\n## 🟦 Highlight Phase — Reflection Notes\n\nGoal: Identify patterns and strong practices you noticed while testing or reading different versions of filter logic.\n\n---\n\n### 🧠 What You Observed\n\nObservation 1:\nSome filters returned `undefined` when no match was found.\nExample or Evidence: In one version, songs without a genre made the array include blank spots.\nWhy It Matters: This can confuse users because the UI might show “empty cards.”\n\n---\n\nObservation 2:\nA clear conditional (`if (song.genre === \"Pop\")`) was easier to understand than nested checks.\nExample or Evidence: The AI’s first example used `if (song.genre && song.genre === \"Pop\")`, which felt longer but safer.\nWhy It Matters: Readable conditionals help debugging later.\n\n---\n\nObservation 3:\nFunctions that used lowercase comparisons (`toLowerCase()`) worked better across cases.\nExample or Evidence: When I typed \"pop\" instead of \"Pop\", the lowercase version still matched correctly.\nWhy It Matters: Makes the function more inclusive and reliable.\n\n---\n\nObservation 4:\nStarting with an empty array made the logic easier to follow.\nExample or Evidence: The AI used `const results = []` before mapping.\nWhy It Matters: Keeps your code predictable and easy to expand.\n\n---\n\nObservation 5:\nHandling missing fields prevented errors.\nExample or Evidence: One song had no genre (\"\"), but some versions crashed without that check.\nWhy It Matters: Defensive code keeps apps accessible and stable.\n\n---\n\n### 🪄 My “Rules of Thumb”\n\n1. Always check that the property exists before comparing.\n2. Use consistent lowercase comparisons for fairness and inclusivity.\n3. Keep conditions simple and easy to read — one idea per `if`.\n\n---\n\n### 💬 Reflection Prompt\n\nHow could filter logic unintentionally leave out songs, artists, or genres?\nWhat’s one change you’d make to make your filters more fair?\n\nSample student reflection:\n\"I realized that filters can unintentionally ignore songs without a genre, even if they’re valid entries. I’d change my code to include them with a label like ‘Uncategorized’ so users still see them.\"\n\n---\n\n### 🧩 Bonus: Connect to Practice\n\nFront-End Developer: Writes filters that are clear, consistent, and easy to maintain.\nData Analyst: Uses conditions to segment data fairly without bias.\nUX Designer: Ensures users understand what’s being filtered out or included.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ My Next Step\n\n[ ] Rewrite my filter function with improved readability.\n[ ] Add a check for missing or undefined data.\n[ ] Write comments explaining each conditional.\n\n---\n\n### 🧾 Optional Extension\n\nTry turning your highlights into a coding guideline your team could reuse in the next project.\n\nExample:\n\"When filtering data, always handle missing values, use lowercase checks, and name your conditions clearly.\"\n",
          "folderId": "0",
          "active": true
        }
